@node Cost estimation
@section Cost estimation

Cost estimation in @samp{gptel-plus} has two complementary aspects: an @emph{ex ante} estimate computed before you send a request, and an @emph{ex post} calculation reported after the response arrives.

@menu
* Header line display::
* Ex post cost calculation::
* Cost warning::
@end menu

@node Header line display
@subsection Header line display

When @samp{gptel-plus} is loaded, it replaces the standard gptel header line with an extended version that includes a clickable @samp{[Cost: $X.XX]} indicator.  This indicator shows the estimated cost of sending the current prompt, updated dynamically as you type or modify the context.  Clicking the cost indicator opens the gptel transient menu.

The estimate accounts for three components:

@enumerate
@item
The words in the current buffer (from the beginning up to point, or in the active region if one exists).
@item
The words in all context files and buffers.
@item
A fixed assumed output length.
@end enumerate

Each word count is multiplied by @code{gptel-plus-tokens-per-word} and then by the model's per-token pricing (read from the @code{:input-cost} and @code{:output-cost} properties that gptel stores on the model symbol).  If no pricing information is available for the current model, the indicator shows @samp{[Cost: N/A]}.

Note that the cost estimate does not account for images or region-based context additions.  The estimate is approximate; the @emph{ex post} calculation (@ref{Ex post cost calculation}) provides the precise figure.

@node Ex post cost calculation
@subsection Ex post cost calculation

After each gptel request completes, @samp{gptel-plus} reports the exact cost in the echo area (e.g., @samp{Cost of request: $0.0042}).

To obtain the exact token counts, @samp{gptel-plus} temporarily sets @code{gptel-log-level} to @code{info} before the request is sent, which causes gptel to log the full request and response data to the @samp{*gptel-log*} buffer.  After the response arrives, @samp{gptel-plus} parses this log to extract the input and output token counts, computes the cost using the model's pricing, and then restores the original log level and kills the log buffer.

This mechanism handles concurrent requests correctly: if multiple requests are in flight simultaneously, the log level is only restored after the last one completes.

@strong{Note:} This feature currently works only with Anthropic models, because it relies on the Anthropic streaming event format (@samp{message_start} and @samp{message_delta} events).  For other providers, the @emph{ex ante} estimate is still available.

@node Cost warning
@subsection Cost warning

Before every call to @code{gptel-send}, @samp{gptel-plus} checks whether the estimated cost exceeds @code{gptel-plus-cost-warning-threshold} (@ref{Cost estimation parameters}).  If it does, a @samp{y-or-n-p} prompt asks you to confirm.  If you decline, a second prompt offers to clear the context (via @code{gptel-context-remove-all}), since a large context is typically the reason for a high cost.  Declining both prompts cancels the request with a @code{user-error}.

@node Context persistence
@section Context persistence

@findex gptel-plus-save-file-context
@findex gptel-plus-restore-file-context

By default, the list of context files you add to a gptel session is lost when you kill the buffer.  @samp{gptel-plus} provides two commands to persist and restore this context.

The command @code{gptel-plus-save-file-context} (@samp{M-x gptel-plus-save-file-context}) saves the current @code{gptel-context} to the file you are visiting.  In Org mode buffers, the context is stored as a @samp{GPTEL_CONTEXT} property on the first heading.  In Markdown mode buffers, it is stored as a file-local variable.  If a saved context already exists, you are asked to confirm the overwrite.  The command signals an error if the buffer is neither Org nor Markdown.

The command @code{gptel-plus-restore-file-context} (@samp{M-x gptel-plus-restore-file-context}) reads the saved context from the file and re-adds each file to the gptel context via @code{gptel-context-add-file}.  If a context is already active, you are asked to confirm the overwrite.  Files that no longer exist or are not readable are skipped, with a message listing the missing paths.

@node Context file management
@section Context file management

@findex gptel-plus-list-context-files
@findex gptel-plus-toggle-mark
@findex gptel-plus-remove-flagged-context-files
@findex gptel-plus-refresh-context-files-buffer

The command @code{gptel-plus-list-context-files} (@samp{M-x gptel-plus-list-context-files}) opens a dedicated @samp{*gptel context files*} buffer listing all files in the current gptel context, sorted by size in descending order.  Each entry shows the file's size in kilobytes and its path (abbreviated with @samp{~/} where applicable).  If there are no files in the context, a message is displayed instead.

The buffer uses @code{gptel-context-files-mode}, a special read-only major mode with the following key bindings:

@multitable {aaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Command
@tab Description
@item @samp{x}
@tab @code{gptel-plus-toggle-mark}
@tab Toggle the removal flag on a file
@item @samp{D}
@tab @code{gptel-plus-remove-flagged-context-files}
@tab Remove all flagged files
@item @samp{g}
@tab @code{gptel-plus-refresh-context-files-buffer}
@tab Refresh the listing
@item @samp{q}
@tab @code{kill-current-buffer}
@tab Close the buffer
@end multitable

The workflow is similar to dired's flagging mechanism: move to a file entry, press @samp{x} to toggle its flag (the marker changes from @samp{[ ]} to @samp{[X]}), repeat for other files, then press @samp{D} to remove all flagged files from the context at once.  The cost estimate is updated automatically after removal.

@node Automatic mode activation
@section Automatic mode activation

@findex gptel-plus-enable-gptel-in-org
@findex gptel-plus-enable-gptel-in-markdown

When you save a gptel conversation, gptel stores session metadata in the file (as Org properties or as Markdown file-local variables).  However, reopening that file does not automatically re-enable @code{gptel-mode}.  @samp{gptel-plus} provides hook functions that detect these markers and activate @code{gptel-mode} automatically.

The function @code{gptel-plus-enable-gptel-in-org} checks for gptel-related Org properties (such as @samp{GPTEL_SYSTEM}, @samp{GPTEL_BACKEND}, @samp{GPTEL_MODEL}, and others) and enables @code{gptel-mode} if any are found.

The function @code{gptel-plus-enable-gptel-in-markdown} checks for gptel-related file-local variables (such as @code{gptel-mode}, @code{gptel-model}, @code{gptel--backend-name}, and @code{gptel--bounds}) and enables @code{gptel-mode} if any are present.

To use these, add the appropriate hooks to your configuration:

@lisp
(add-hook 'org-mode-hook #'gptel-plus-enable-gptel-in-org)
(add-hook 'markdown-mode-hook #'gptel-plus-enable-gptel-in-markdown)
@end lisp

When activating @code{gptel-mode} automatically, @samp{gptel-plus} takes care not to mark the buffer as modified (which would otherwise happen as a side effect of enabling the mode).  It also disables @code{breadcrumb-mode} if active, since it conflicts with the gptel header line.

