@node Cost computation
@section Cost computation

@findex gptel-plus-get-total-cost
@findex gptel-plus-get-input-cost
@findex gptel-plus-get-output-cost
@findex gptel-plus-normalize-cost
@findex gptel-plus-update-context-cost

The function @code{gptel-plus-get-total-cost} returns the estimated total cost (in dollars) of sending the current prompt, or @code{nil} if cost information is unavailable for the active model.  It combines the input and output costs and normalizes the result.  This is the function used by the header line display (@ref{Header line display}).

The function @code{gptel-plus-get-input-cost} returns the estimated input cost, combining the buffer content cost with the cached context cost.  The function @code{gptel-plus-get-output-cost} returns the estimated output cost based on @code{gptel-plus-tokens-in-output} (@ref{Cost estimation parameters}).

The function @code{gptel-plus-normalize-cost} converts a raw cost figure (expressed in cost-per-million-tokens units) into a dollar amount by dividing by one million.

The function @code{gptel-plus-update-context-cost} recalculates and caches the cost of the current context files.  It is called automatically (via advice) whenever files are added to or removed from the context, and whenever the model or backend changes.  You generally do not need to call it directly, but it is available if you modify @code{gptel-context} programmatically.

@node Word counting
@section Word counting

@findex gptel-plus-count-words-in-buffer
@findex gptel-plus-count-words-in-context

The function @code{gptel-plus-count-words-in-buffer} returns the word count for the portion of the buffer that will be sent to the model.  If a region is active, it counts words in the region; otherwise, it counts from the beginning of the buffer to point (since gptel sends everything up to point).

The function @code{gptel-plus-count-words-in-context} iterates over all entries in @code{gptel-context} and sums their word counts.  Buffer entries are counted directly; file entries are read into a temporary buffer for counting.  Binary files and unreadable files are skipped.  Dead buffers (killed since they were added to the context) are also skipped.

@node Context detection
@section Context detection

@findex gptel-plus-file-has-gptel-local-variable-p
@findex gptel-plus-file-has-gptel-org-property-p

The function @code{gptel-plus-file-has-gptel-local-variable-p} returns non-nil if the current buffer has any gptel-related file-local variable set.  It checks for @code{gptel-mode}, @code{gptel-model}, @code{gptel--backend-name}, and @code{gptel--bounds}.

The function @code{gptel-plus-file-has-gptel-org-property-p} returns non-nil if the current Org buffer has any gptel-related property at the beginning of the file.  It checks for @samp{GPTEL_SYSTEM}, @samp{GPTEL_BACKEND}, @samp{GPTEL_MODEL}, @samp{GPTEL_TEMPERATURE}, @samp{GPTEL_MAX_TOKENS}, and @samp{GPTEL_NUM_MESSAGES_TO_SEND}.

Both functions are used by the automatic mode activation hooks (@ref{Automatic mode activation}) and can also be called independently to test whether a file contains gptel data.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@menu
* Cost shows ``N/A''::
* Ex post cost not reported::
* Context restore skips files::
@end menu

@node Cost shows ``N/A''
@section Cost shows ``N/A''

The header line displays @samp{[Cost: N/A]} when the current model does not have @code{:input-cost} or @code{:output-cost} properties set.  These properties are defined by gptel when you register a backend.  If you are using a custom backend, ensure that the model symbols have these properties set (the values are in dollars per million tokens).

@node Ex post cost not reported
@section Ex post cost not reported

The @emph{ex post} cost calculation currently works only with Anthropic models.  For other providers, the echo area message will not appear.  The @emph{ex ante} estimate in the header line is provider-agnostic and works for any model that has pricing properties set.

If the cost calculation fails (e.g., due to unexpected log format changes in a new gptel version), a message of the form @samp{gptel-plus: failed to calculate cost: ...} will appear in the echo area instead of silently failing.

@node Context restore skips files
@section Context restore skips files

When restoring a saved context, @code{gptel-plus-restore-file-context} checks that each file exists and is readable before adding it.  If files have been moved, renamed, or deleted since the context was saved, they are skipped and a message lists the missing paths.  Update the saved context by saving it again after correcting your file paths.

